# CDK boot policy (roadmap 2.9), packed into the ramdisk as policy.rules.
# Every grant, policy edit, and gateway listing change is checked against
# these rules before the operator is asked; a change that would break one
# is refused. If this file is malformed, CDK refuses every change.
#
#   deny <label>                no agent may hold any handle on such objects
#   separate <a> <b>            no agent may hold handles reaching both
#   approval <label>            exec/send/write/delete on such objects must
#                               carry the human-approval constraint
#   label <object-or-kind> <l>  attach a label to an object id or kind
#   require sealed-store        peer messages and imports need the TPM-sealed
#                               store (roadmap 4.5)
#
# Automatic labels: tool, model, credential (the gateway adds a key),
# remote (requests leave this machine).

# Distributed capabilities (roadmap 3.5): accept peers' messages and
# capabilities only while the TPM-sealed store (roadmap 4.5) holds the
# revocation and replay records — so a boot without it (TPM removed or
# disabled, BIOS instead of UEFI) cannot be used to replay them. Lab setups
# without a TPM can remove this line; their records then last one boot.
require sealed-store

# Anything that spends a credential or leaves the machine needs a human
# decision on every use.
approval credential
approval remote

# Example: keep agents that read customer data away from tools and
# off-machine models.
#   label obj-2 customer-data
#   separate customer-data tool
#   separate customer-data remote
